windowOkT -- popups stroke 3 (SEQ 768, 2026-11-24; Tony's ruling 4 on docs/popupsRecon.md): OK and Cancel. Tester's shape, a
delta of windowPopupT. No window is shown. okHost has two fields, okA "first" and okB "second", each opening okPop at itself.
okPop has two rows that pick their own text (popupPick), an OK (popupOk) and a Cancel (popupCancel). Every click in okPop is a
real mouse-up in okPop's own window, so OK and Cancel close the window they were clicked in.
Read: the text of okA and okB in the DEFINITION, and okA's height there (a write must leave a field's attributes alone); how many popups are open (targets); okHost's window against the bitmap read
just after the popup was opened, the clicked field already outlined (displayDiff: differing 0 is equal pixel for pixel); and whether a window still holds okPop (readWindow refuses by name
when none does).
K-0  nothing yet: first, second; the page equal to itself.
K-1  okA opens the popup, red is picked, CANCEL: closed, nothing written, the page equal pixel for pixel.
K-2  the same with the close button (windowClose): the same.
K-3  OK with nothing picked: closed, nothing written and it says so, the page equal.
K-4  red picked, OK: okA's text is red in the definition; the popup is closed; okHost's window is laid out again in place
     (not closed, not moved) and its page differs from the one read before.
K-5  opened on okB, red picked then blue: the later pick wins; okB is written, okA keeps red.
K-6  ERRANT: popupOk fired by a field that is in no popup (okLoose, in okHost): refused by name, nothing written.
